{"content_hash": "4cc1faefd684c2d9", "version_id": 463, "article_id": 47, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Flock cuts 270 jobs as camera backlash grows", "title_zh": "Flock 裁员270人，摄像头争议持续升温", "has_translation": true, "topic": "tech", "source": "guardian_tech", "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/09/flock-job-cuts", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1562739974-e15519c03d87?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8OHx8c3VydmVpbGxhbmNlJTIwc3RhcnR1cCUyMGZsb2NrfGVufDB8MHx8fDE3OTE2NjU4NTR8MA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Kaspars Eglitis on Unsplash", "author": "Kaspars Eglitis", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@kasparseglitis?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/bird-on-white-cctv-camera-BCMCMuTISio?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(321, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(359, 46%, 48%)", "label": "TECH", "seed": "9651cefffeca"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-09", "word_count": 171, "reading_minutes": 0.9, "lead": "Flock Safety is an AI surveillance company that makes cameras and license-plate readers. It is now cutting jobs because many people and lawmakers oppose its products.", "lead_zh": "Flock Safety 是一家AI监控公司，生产摄像头和车牌识别器。由于许多人和立法者反对其产品，公司正在裁员。", "preview": "Flock Safety plans to cut about 18% of its workers, or roughly 270 people, according to sources. The job cuts follow a voluntary buyout prog", "preview_zh": "据消息人士称，Flock Safety 计划裁减约18%的员工，即大约270人。此次裁员是在自愿买断计划之后进行的，员工将在月底离职。Flock 约有1500名员工，拒绝置评。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 456, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/47/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 83.562, "size_bytes": 505152, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 201, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@fc48a26/content/data/versions/463.json", "paragraphs": ["Flock Safety plans to cut about 18% of its workers, or roughly 270 people, according to sources. The job cuts follow a voluntary buyout program, and employees will leave at the end of the month. Flock, which has about 1,500 employees, declined to comment.", "The Atlanta-based startup was founded in 2017 and has raised over $950 million from investors. Its latest round, led by Andreessen Horowitz in March, valued the company at $7.5 billion. Flock provides cameras and software to more than 4,800 law enforcement agencies and nearly 1,000 businesses.", "However, Flock faces growing scrutiny over privacy. Its network of about 120,000 AI-powered cameras across 49 states reads license plates automatically. A recent Reuters/Ipsos poll found that 38% of Americans support Flock cameras in their communities, while 47% oppose them.", "Public backlash is increasing. In September, Florida banned automated license plate readers on state highways because of privacy risks. Home Depot investors also asked for reviews of the retailer's partnerships with surveillance vendors. Flock also faces a privacy lawsuit in Virginia."], "paragraphs_zh": ["据消息人士称，Flock Safety 计划裁减约18%的员工，即大约270人。此次裁员是在自愿买断计划之后进行的，员工将在月底离职。Flock 约有1500名员工，拒绝置评。", "这家总部位于亚特兰大的初创公司成立于2017年，已从投资者那里筹集了超过9.5亿美元。其最新一轮融资由 Andreessen Horowitz 在3月领投，公司估值达75亿美元。Flock 向超过4800个执法机构和近1000家企业提供摄像头和软件。", "然而，Flock 因隐私问题面临越来越多的审查。其在49个州部署的约12万台AI摄像头自动读取车牌。最近的路透社/益普索民调发现，38%的美国人支持在社区使用Flock摄像头，而47%的人反对。", "公众的反对声浪日益高涨。9月，佛罗里达州以隐私风险为由，禁止在州高速公路上使用自动车牌识别器。家得宝的投资者也要求审查该零售商与监控供应商的合作关系。Flock 还在弗吉尼亚州面临一项隐私诉讼。"], "body": "Flock Safety plans to cut about 18% of its workers, or roughly 270 people, according to sources. The job cuts follow a voluntary buyout program, and employees will leave at the end of the month. Flock, which has about 1,500 employees, declined to comment.\n\nThe Atlanta-based startup was founded in 2017 and has raised over $950 million from investors. Its latest round, led by Andreessen Horowitz in March, valued the company at $7.5 billion. Flock provides cameras and software to more than 4,800 law enforcement agencies and nearly 1,000 businesses.\n\nHowever, Flock faces growing scrutiny over privacy. Its network of about 120,000 AI-powered cameras across 49 states reads license plates automatically. A recent Reuters/Ipsos poll found that 38% of Americans support Flock cameras in their communities, while 47% oppose them.\n\nPublic backlash is increasing. In September, Florida banned automated license plate readers on state highways because of privacy risks. Home Depot investors also asked for reviews of the retailer's partnerships with surveillance vendors. Flock also faces a privacy lawsuit in Virginia.", "body_zh": "据消息人士称，Flock Safety 计划裁减约18%的员工，即大约270人。此次裁员是在自愿买断计划之后进行的，员工将在月底离职。Flock 约有1500名员工，拒绝置评。\n\n这家总部位于亚特兰大的初创公司成立于2017年，已从投资者那里筹集了超过9.5亿美元。其最新一轮融资由 Andreessen Horowitz 在3月领投，公司估值达75亿美元。Flock 向超过4800个执法机构和近1000家企业提供摄像头和软件。\n\n然而，Flock 因隐私问题面临越来越多的审查。其在49个州部署的约12万台AI摄像头自动读取车牌。最近的路透社/益普索民调发现，38%的美国人支持在社区使用Flock摄像头，而47%的人反对。\n\n公众的反对声浪日益高涨。9月，佛罗里达州以隐私风险为由，禁止在州高速公路上使用自动车牌识别器。家得宝的投资者也要求审查该零售商与监控供应商的合作关系。Flock 还在弗吉尼亚州面临一项隐私诉讼。", "text": "Flock Safety plans to cut about 18% of its workers, or roughly 270 people, according to sources. The job cuts follow a voluntary buyout program, and employees will leave at the end of the month. Flock, which has about 1,500 employees, declined to comment. The Atlanta-based startup was founded in 2017 and has raised over $950 million from investors. Its latest round, led by Andreessen Horowitz in March, valued the company at $7.5 billion. Flock provides cameras and software to more than 4,800 law enforcement agencies and nearly 1,000 businesses. However, Flock faces growing scrutiny over privacy. Its network of about 120,000 AI-powered cameras across 49 states reads license plates automatically. A recent Reuters/Ipsos poll found that 38% of Americans support Flock cameras in their communities, while 47% oppose them. Public backlash is increasing. In September, Florida banned automated license plate readers on state highways because of privacy risks. Home Depot investors also asked for reviews of the retailer's partnerships with surveillance vendors. Flock also faces a privacy lawsuit in Virginia.", "vocab": [{"word": "surveillance", "pos": "noun", "zh": "监控", "note": "常与 camera, system 搭配", "phonetic": "sә:'veilәns", "en": "close observation of a person or group (usually by the police)"}, {"word": "backlash", "pos": "noun", "zh": "强烈反对", "note": "指公众的抵制", "phonetic": "'bæklæʃ", "en": "an adverse reaction to some political or social occurrence"}, {"word": "scrutiny", "pos": "noun", "zh": "审查，仔细检查", "note": "常与 under 连用", "phonetic": "'skru:tini", "en": "a prolonged intense look"}, {"word": "privacy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "隐私", "note": "隐私相关话题常用", "phonetic": "'praivәsi", "en": "the quality of being secluded from the presence or view of others"}, {"word": "license plate", "pos": "noun", "zh": "车牌", "note": "美式英语", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "voluntary", "pos": "adj", "zh": "自愿的", "note": "voluntary buyout 自愿买断", "phonetic": "'vɒlәntәri", "en": "composition (often improvised) for a solo instrument (especially solo organ) and not a regular part of a religious…"}, {"word": "buyout", "pos": "noun", "zh": "买断", "note": "指公司出钱让员工离职", "phonetic": "ˈbaɪaʊt", "en": "acquisition of a company by purchasing a controlling percentage of its stock"}, {"word": "startup", "pos": "noun", "zh": "初创公司", "note": "常指科技创业公司", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "lawsuit", "pos": "noun", "zh": "诉讼", "note": "法律语境常用", "phonetic": "'lɒ:sju:t", "en": "a comprehensive term for any proceeding in a court of law whereby an individual seeks a legal remedy"}, {"word": "oppose", "pos": "verb", "zh": "反对", "note": "oppose + 名词/动名词", "phonetic": "ә'pәuz", "en": "be against; express opposition to"}], "title_original": "AI surveillance startup Flock to cut several hundred jobs amid backlash, sources say", "summary_original": "Company, which grown rapidly in recent years, has been under scrutiny as privacy concerns grow\nFlock Safety plans to shed about 18% of its employees, people with direct ⁠knowledge of the plans said on Thursday, as the maker of\nAI\n-powered\nsurveillance\ncameras and license-plate readers faces\nmounting opposition\nto its products from communities and ⁠lawmakers.\nThe people said the job ⁠cuts came after ​a voluntary buyout program and were expected to affect roughly 270 employees at Flock, a surveillance technology startup that has seen rapid growth in recent ⁠years. Employees will leave the company at the end of the month.\nContinue reading...", "body_original": "AI surveillance startup Flock to cut several hundred jobs amid backlash, sources say\nCompany, which grown rapidly in recent years, has been under scrutiny as privacy concerns grow\nFlock Safety plans to shed about 18% of its employees, people with direct ⁠knowledge of the plans said on Thursday, as the maker of\nAI\n-powered\nsurveillance\ncameras and license-plate readers faces\nmounting opposition\nto its products from communities and ⁠lawmakers.\nThe people said the job ⁠cuts came after ​a voluntary buyout program and were expected to affect roughly 270 employees at Flock, a surveillance technology startup that has seen rapid growth in recent ⁠years. Employees will leave the company at the end of the month.\nSources declined to be identified as the company has yet to make the plan public.\nFlock declined ⁠to comment.\nIn September, Flock announced a voluntary separation program, asking employees to apply if they wanted to ​leave the company, as Wired reported. The ‌company has about 1,500 employees.\nFlock faces ‌\ngrowing scrutiny\nfrom regulators, lawmakers and privacy advocates, despite drawing strong investor interest among venture capitalists. A ‌recent Reuters/Ipsos poll showed Americans are divided on whether Flock cameras are a good idea. About 38% of the country supports the use of Flock cameras in their community, compared with 47% who oppose their use.\nAcross 49 states, Flock’s network of about 120,000 AI-powered cameras, affixed to streets and highways, automatically read license plates and record vehicles as they pass.\nThe company, based in Atlanta and founded in ‌2017, has raised more than $950m from venture capital investors, with its latest $275m round in March led by Andreessen Horowitz valuing the company at $7.5bn. The funds have supported a ​new US manufacturing facility and an upcoming push into drone products, according to the company.\nFlock provides cameras and software to more than 4,800 law enforcement agencies and nearly 1,000 businesses. The company says its technology helps investigate and solve crimes, but it has come under increasing scrutiny over alleged privacy violations and data-sharing practices.\nPublic backlash ⁠has grown. In September, Florida banned automated license plate readers from state highways, ​citing privacy risks tied to Flock’s ​cameras.\nHome Depot investors recently demanded reviews ​of the retailer’s partnerships with surveillance technology vendors after reports that Flock’s data was used ​in immigration enforcement.\nFlock also ‌faces legal challenges, such ​as a privacy ​lawsuit in Virginia alleging that the widespread deployment of its AI-powered cameras constitutes warrantless surveillance.\nTogether with AI datacenters, Flock cameras have emerged as a flashpoint ahead of November’s midterm elections, with candidates increasingly attacking their opponents by tying them to the technology.\nExplore more on these topics\nSurveillance\nAI (artificial intelligence)\nnews\nShare\nReuse this content", "fetched_at": "2026-10-10T20:57:32.679444", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Flock", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.488, "sm": 100, "em": 487, "cs": 0, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Safety", "s": 0.5, "e": 0.912, "sm": 500, "em": 912, "cs": 